1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,139
This number keeps breaking computers.

2
00:00:02,229 --> 00:00:08,314
2,147,483,647.

3
00:00:08,404 --> 00:00:10,548
It made YouTube rebuild its view counter.

4
00:00:10,638 --> 00:00:14,576
It made regulators order airlines to switch a jet off and on again.

5
00:00:14,666 --> 00:00:18,856
Its bigger twin is the prime suspect in the loss of a NASA spacecraft.

6
00:00:18,946 --> 00:00:22,220
And in January 2038, it's coming for the clocks.

7
00:00:22,310 --> 00:00:25,990
All for the same reason. Somebody thought: we'll never count that high.

8
00:00:26,990 --> 00:00:31,565
Computers store numbers in bits: tiny switches that are either zero or one.

9
00:00:31,655 --> 00:00:35,702
For decades, the standard size for a whole number was 32 of them.

10
00:00:35,792 --> 00:00:39,811
32 switches give about 4.3 billion combinations.

11
00:00:39,901 --> 00:00:44,386
But programs need negative numbers too, so one switch is used for the sign.

12
00:00:44,476 --> 00:00:54,205
That leaves 31 for the value. Set them all to one, and you get 2,147,483,647.

13
00:00:54,295 --> 00:00:57,888
It works like an old car odometer with every digit at nine.

14
00:00:57,978 --> 00:01:00,162
Add one more, and everything rolls over.

15
00:01:00,252 --> 00:01:03,704
Except here, it doesn't roll to zero. The sign switch flips,

16
00:01:03,794 --> 00:01:07,358
and the biggest positive number becomes the most negative one.

17
00:01:07,448 --> 00:01:12,181
That's an integer overflow. And it has happened in some very expensive places.

18
00:01:13,181 --> 00:01:18,139
December 2014. Gangnam Style is the most-watched video on YouTube,

19
00:01:18,229 --> 00:01:21,515
and its view count is closing in on 2.1 billion.

20
00:01:21,605 --> 00:01:23,280
Then YouTube posts a message.

21
00:01:23,370 --> 00:01:28,173
It never thought a video would be watched more times than a 32-bit number can hold.

22
00:01:28,263 --> 00:01:30,717
Headlines said Gangnam Style broke YouTube.

23
00:01:30,807 --> 00:01:34,962
The truth is better. Days later, YouTube said the post was an Easter egg.

24
00:01:35,052 --> 00:01:40,490
Its engineers had seen the number coming, and moved the counter to 64 bits months earlier.

25
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:43,149
The new limit is over nine quintillion views.

26
00:01:43,239 --> 00:01:46,965
That's the happy version of this story: someone was watching the number.

27
00:01:47,965 --> 00:01:49,697
The next one was found in a lab.

28
00:01:49,787 --> 00:01:55,340
In 2015, Boeing told regulators about a problem in the 787 Dreamliner.

29
00:01:55,430 --> 00:02:00,898
Each of its generators is managed by a control unit. Inside its software is a counter.

30
00:02:00,988 --> 00:02:05,739
After 248 days of continuous power, that counter overflows,

31
00:02:05,829 --> 00:02:08,059
and the unit drops into failsafe mode.

32
00:02:08,149 --> 00:02:14,045
Power them all up together, and they all overflow together. The plane could lose all of its AC power.

33
00:02:14,135 --> 00:02:17,827
The FAA never published the counter's details. But do the maths.

34
00:02:17,917 --> 00:02:25,655
Count hundredths of a second in a signed 32-bit number, and you run out after 248.55 days.

35
00:02:25,745 --> 00:02:31,939
The official fix, until new software arrived: switch the power off and on again before the counter gets there.

36
00:02:32,029 --> 00:02:34,035
It was caught in testing, not in the air.

37
00:02:35,035 --> 00:02:37,178
A NASA spacecraft wasn't so lucky.

38
00:02:37,268 --> 00:02:42,209
Deep Impact launched in 2005, and famously fired a probe into a comet.

39
00:02:42,299 --> 00:02:45,663
Eight years later, in August 2013, it went silent.

40
00:02:45,753 --> 00:02:51,993
NASA never confirmed the exact cause, but it found a potential problem with how the computer tagged time.

41
00:02:52,083 --> 00:03:00,084
Outside engineers noticed something. Count tenths of a second from the first of January 2000, in a 32-bit number,

42
00:03:00,174 --> 00:03:05,533
and you hit the limit, about 4.29 billion, on the 11th of August 2013.

43
00:03:05,623 --> 00:03:07,519
The same week contact was lost.

44
00:03:07,609 --> 00:03:14,612
The suspected result: the computer kept rebooting, the craft lost its orientation, and its antennas turned away from Earth.

45
00:03:14,702 --> 00:03:17,035
A month later, the mission was declared over.

46
00:03:18,035 --> 00:03:19,391
It hits money, too.

47
00:03:19,481 --> 00:03:25,484
In May 2021, one share of Berkshire Hathaway cost more than 420,000 dollars.

48
00:03:25,574 --> 00:03:31,439
Some Nasdaq price feeds stored prices as 32-bit numbers, with four decimal places.

49
00:03:31,529 --> 00:03:38,503
So the highest price they could carry was $429,496. And change.

50
00:03:38,593 --> 00:03:44,912
As the share closed in, Nasdaq stopped broadcasting its price on those feeds until the software was upgraded.

51
00:03:45,912 --> 00:03:47,540
Which brings us to the big one.

52
00:03:47,630 --> 00:03:53,161
Many computers keep time by counting the seconds since the first of January 1970.

53
00:03:53,251 --> 00:03:58,234
It's called Unix time, and for decades it was stored in a signed 32-bit number.

54
00:03:58,324 --> 00:03:59,768
You already know its limit.

55
00:03:59,858 --> 00:04:08,922
That many seconds after 1970 lands on the 19th of January 2038, at seven seconds past 3:14 in the morning, UTC.

56
00:04:09,012 --> 00:04:14,891
One second later, the counter flips negative, and an unpatched system thinks it's December 1901.

57
00:04:14,981 --> 00:04:17,178
This is the Year 2038 problem.

58
00:04:17,268 --> 00:04:21,426
The fix is the one YouTube used: store time in 64 bits.

59
00:04:21,516 --> 00:04:24,375
That lasts about 292 billion years.

60
00:04:24,465 --> 00:04:27,562
Most modern phones and laptops already made the switch.

61
00:04:27,652 --> 00:04:34,044
The risk is in older and embedded systems: machines built to run for decades, that are hard to update.

62
00:04:34,134 --> 00:04:37,254
Engineers have a little over eleven years to find them.

63
00:04:38,254 --> 00:04:40,742
Every one of these stories started the same way.

64
00:04:40,832 --> 00:04:44,402
Someone picked a size for a number, and it seemed impossibly big.

65
00:04:44,492 --> 00:04:49,816
Two billion views. Eight months without a reboot. A share worth 400,000 dollars.

66
00:04:49,906 --> 00:04:52,330
The number didn't change. The world grew into it.

67
00:04:52,420 --> 00:04:57,459
So when someone says we'll never count that high, remember: eventually, something does.
